{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>On the day of the first month, on the first of the month, you shall set up the Mishkan, the Tent of Meeting.</b> In the parashah the language of setting up is written three times: first, 'you shall set up the Mishkan'; second, 'the Mishkan was set up'; third, 'and Moses set up.' And the Sages said (Leviticus Rabbah 52:4) that Israel and all the wise of heart would set up the Mishkan and could not set it up; the Holy One, blessed be He, said: You engage in setting it up, and it stands on its own — as it says, 'was set up' — on its own is what it implies. And there is in this an allusion for all the deeds that a person does: for the hand of man is too short to complete them from beginning to end; rather, man begins and HaShem completes through him. And so the Sages said (Kiddushin 30a) regarding the evil inclination: were the Holy One, blessed be He, not to help him, he could not prevail against it, etc. And He commanded that the setting up of the Mishkan be through Moses, alluding to the words of the midrash that says that the world was created in the merit of Moses, as it says, 'בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים' — in the merit of Moses, who was called רֵאשִׁית, as it says (Deuteronomy 33:21) 'וַיַּרְא רֵאשִׁית לוֹ.' And if so, it is as though Moses set up the world; so too the Mishkan, which was built in the pattern of the world, was set up through Moses. And just as 'בְּרֵאשִׁית' — the world was created, so on 'the day of the first month' the Mishkan was set up. And according to the Sages (Torat Kohanim Shemini 1) this day was the eighth day of the inauguration, and that day took ten crowns: first for the act of Creation, etc.; see in the Yalkut, parashat Shemini (612)." "And these three settings up — some commentators said that they correspond to the three Temples. And I have what resembles a proof for their words: for the first and second Temples depended on the merit of Israel, for through their merit and their repentance they merited this building. Therefore it says 'תָּקִים' and 'יָקֶם,' for these speak of a setting up that depends on human action. But the third Temple does not depend on merit, for even in a generation that is entirely guilty the Redeemer will come, and if so it does not depend on human action but will be built on its own. Therefore it says 'הוּקַם' — on its own is what it implies."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:51.993127808Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}